Declare getline() with ssize_t, its actual POSIX return type.

V emits its own CRT prototype for getline using `isize`, which is
`typedef ptrdiff_t isize`. POSIX declares getline as returning ssize_t. On
64-bit Darwin both are `long`, so nothing is noticed, but on 32-bit PowerPC
ptrdiff_t is `int` while ssize_t is `long` -- distinct types even at the same
width -- so the prototype conflicts with the system (or MacportsLegacySupport)
declaration and the compile fails with "conflicting types for 'getline'".

This block is V's replacement for the libc headers, so it is emitted before any
system header: ssize_t is not in scope here and naming it alone gives "unknown
type name 'ssize_t'". <sys/types.h> is where POSIX puts ssize_t, and it declares
types only, so including it ahead of the manual libc prototypes cannot clash
with them. V includes it itself a little further down, just too late to help.

Windows keeps the previous declaration, since it has no POSIX getline, no
<sys/types.h> ssize_t, and MSVC does not necessarily provide ssize_t at all.

--- vlib/v/gen/c/cheaders.v.original
+++ vlib/v/gen/c/cheaders.v
@@ -665,7 +665,12 @@
 V_CRT_LINKAGE char * V_CRT_CALL strrchr(const char *str, int c);
 V_CRT_LINKAGE char * V_CRT_CALL strstr(const char *haystack, const char *needle);
 V_CRT_LINKAGE int V_CRT_CALL fseek(FILE *stream, long offset, int whence);
+#if defined(_WIN32) || defined(_WIN64)
 V_CRT_LINKAGE isize V_CRT_CALL getline(char **lineptr, size_t *n, FILE *stream);
+#else
+#include <sys/types.h>
+V_CRT_LINKAGE ssize_t V_CRT_CALL getline(char **lineptr, size_t *n, FILE *stream);
+#endif
 #if defined(_WIN32) || defined(_WIN64)
 V_CRT_LINKAGE int V_CRT_CALL _fileno(FILE *stream);
 V_CRT_LINKAGE FILE * V_CRT_CALL _wfopen(const unsigned short *filename, const unsigned short *mode);
